    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          No, not in a typical home pressure washer: most consumer electric and gas units are cold-water machines, and their manuals commonly cap inlet water at roughly 100 to 104°F (about 38 to 40°C). Feed one water from a household water heater, usually set near 120°F or higher, and you are cooking the pump seals and valves that the cool water is supposed to protect. Machines that spray hot water exist, but they heat the water after the pump with a burner or heating element, which is a different and far more expensive tool. For grease and grime on a cold-water machine, the right fix is detergent and dwell time, not a hotter supply.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Can a Regular Pressure Washer Take Hot Water?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For almost every pressure washer sold for home use, the answer is no. These are cold-water machines. The pump is built to move water at roughly the temperature that comes out of an outdoor spigot, and the materials inside it, from the rubber seals to the plastic or aluminum pump head, are chosen with that in mind. The owner's manual will usually list a maximum inlet water temperature, and on consumer electric units that number is commonly about 104°F, sometimes a little lower.
          </p>

          <p>
            That limit is lower than most people expect. A household water heater is typically set around 120°F, which is the setting the Department of Energy suggests for most homes, and many are set hotter. So the idea of running a hose from a laundry sink or a utility tap with the hot side open is not a small stretch of the rules. It puts water 15 to 30 degrees or more above what the pump is rated to handle, continuously, for as long as you clean.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are exceptions, and they matter. Some higher-end gas pressure washers use triplex plunger pumps from industrial pump makers that are rated for warmer inlet water, sometimes 140°F or more depending on the model. Dedicated hot-water pressure washers spray water at well over 150°F. But neither of those describes the electric unit in most garages, and the only reliable source for your machine is its own manual or spec sheet, not a general rule.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers That Matter</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~104°F</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common inlet limit, consumer units</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~120°F</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical water heater setting</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">140°F+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Some triplex pump ratings</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Place to confirm: your manual</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The practical summary is simple. If your pressure washer cost a few hundred dollars, has an axial pump, and connects to a garden hose, treat it as a cold-water machine and feed it cold water. If you think you need hot water for the job in front of you, you are usually right that heat helps, but wrong about where the heat should come from. We will get to the alternatives that work on the machine you already own.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Does Hot Water Damage a Cold-Water Pump?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The incoming water does more than get pushed out of the nozzle. It is also the pump's cooling system and its lubricant. Every piston or plunger in the pump slides through a seal, and those seals rely on a film of cool water to stay pliable, sealed and below the temperature where the rubber starts to break down. Most consumer pumps use nitrile-type seals and O-rings that are fine at tap temperatures and age much faster when they run hot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heat also stacks. Even with cold water going in, a pump warms up whenever water recirculates inside it, which is what happens every time you release the trigger on a machine that keeps running. Start with water that is already near or above the rated limit and the margin disappears. The pump reaches the temperature where seals soften, swell or harden far sooner, and the thermal relief valve, if the machine has one, may start dumping hot water just to protect itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hot water also cavitates more easily. Cavitation is what happens when low pressure on the suction side of the pump lets the water form vapor bubbles that collapse violently against the valves and pump walls. Warmer water has a higher vapor pressure, so it boils at a lower suction pressure, which means a pump fed hot water is more prone to the surging, rattling and erosion that cavitation causes. That is one reason even industrial pumps rated for hot water often want it supplied under pressure rather than drawn.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then there is the hardware itself. Many consumer pumps use plastic manifolds, plastic inlet fittings and plastic quick-connects. Those parts are designed for cool water under a known pressure and can deform, creep or crack when they spend hours warmer than intended. A pump head that has been slightly warped will leak around its seams, and that is rarely something you can fix with a new O-ring.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Cool water is the pump's coolant, not just its fuel.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Raise the inlet temperature and every other source of heat in the pump, from recirculation to friction, pushes the seals past their limit sooner.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Finally, the warranty. Manufacturers state the inlet limit because they know what happens above it, and pump damage from overheated supply water is easy to recognise when a unit comes back for service. Running hot water through a machine that says not to is one of the clearest ways to turn a covered repair into a paid one. On a budget machine, that paid repair often costs enough that replacement makes more sense.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="limit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Do You Find Your Machine's Inlet Temperature Limit?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the manual, usually in the specifications table or the section on connecting the water supply. Look for a line such as maximum inlet water temperature or maximum water supply temperature. On many electric units you will find a figure around 104°F or 40°C. Some manuals phrase it as a warning instead, telling you not to connect to a hot water supply. Either way, that line overrides anything you read online, including this page.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have lost the manual, the model number on the rating plate is enough. Most brands post manuals as downloadable PDFs on their support pages, and the specifications section is usually near the front. Retail listings sometimes include the inlet limit too, but they are less reliable than the manufacturer's own document, so use them only to confirm what the manual says.
          </p>

          <p>
            For gas machines, check the pump rather than the engine. Many mid-range gas units carry an axial pump similar in principle to the ones on electric machines, with a similar inlet limit. Machines with a branded triplex pump may list a higher limit for the pump itself, and the pump maker's spec sheet will show it. Do not assume a higher number just because the engine is bigger or the machine was more expensive.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Machine type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical inlet limit</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Hot supply OK?</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Budget electric, axial pump</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 100-104°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Mid-range electric, induction motor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Commonly about 104°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No, unless manual says otherwise</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gas, axial pump</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often similar to electric</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gas, triplex plunger pump</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies; some rated 140°F or more</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Only within the pump's rating</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hot-water pressure washer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cold supply; heats after the pump</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Feed it cold water</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If the manual gives no number at all, take the cautious reading: cold water only. A pump with no stated limit is not a pump designed for hot supply, it is a pump whose maker assumed you would use a garden hose. The same goes for accessories. Hoses, guns, quick-connects and surface cleaners carry their own temperature ratings, and consumer gear is generally rated for cold-water use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warm" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What About Warm Water, or a Hose Left in the Sun?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Warm water below the stated limit is fine. If your manual says 104°F, water at 80 or 90°F is inside the rating, and the pump will not care. The trouble is that people rarely measure. Mixing hot and cold at a utility sink by feel can easily land above 104°F, because water that feels merely warm on your hand is often already past it. If you are not putting a thermometer in the stream, you are guessing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The more common accidental case is a garden hose that has been lying in the sun. On a hot afternoon, the water sitting in a dark hose can get noticeably hot, sometimes hot enough to sting. The first minute of flow from that hose may be well above the inlet limit. The fix costs nothing: run the hose onto the lawn until the water comes out cool before you connect it, which also flushes out any grit that would otherwise end up on the inlet screen.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rain barrels and tanks have the same problem in summer. A plastic tank in full sun can hold water much warmer than the mains supply, and some pressure washers are not designed to draw from a tank at all. If you supply from a tank, check that your machine allows it, keep the tank shaded, and confirm the water temperature before a long job. Our guide to <a href="/pressure-washer-water-supply-requirements/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">pressure washer water supply requirements</a> covers flow, pressure and tank feeding in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more trap is a pressure washer that has been sitting in the sun itself. The water left inside the pump and hose after the last use will be warm when you start. That is a short pulse rather than a sustained supply problem, and a cool supply will flush it quickly, but it is another reason to store the machine in shade and to start each session by running water through before you switch on the motor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hot-machines" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Are Hot-Water Pressure Washers Different?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A hot-water pressure washer is not a cold-water machine with a hotter hose. It takes a cold supply into the pump like any other pressure washer, then runs the high-pressure water through a heating coil or heat exchanger before it reaches the gun. The heat is usually supplied by a diesel or kerosene burner on commercial units, or by electric heating elements on some smaller ones. The pump never sees hot water, which is exactly the point.
          </p>

          <p>
            That design adds a lot. There is a burner or element, a fuel tank or a heavy electrical supply, a coil, temperature controls and safety cutoffs. Commercial hot-water units commonly heat output water to somewhere around 180 to 200°F, and some can switch to a low-pressure steam mode. Many of the electric hot-water models need a 240-volt circuit, and burner-heated units produce exhaust, so they are used outdoors or with ventilation.
          </p>

          <p>
            They are also priced as professional equipment. A capable hot-water pressure washer generally costs several times what a good consumer electric machine does, often running into the thousands of dollars, and it is heavier, louder and needs more maintenance. For contractors cleaning engine bays, restaurant equipment, fleet vehicles or oily concrete every day, the speed of hot water pays for that. For a homeowner washing a driveway twice a year, it does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you regularly face heavy grease, the honest options are to rent a hot-water unit for the day from an equipment rental shop, or hire a contractor who owns one. Both cost far less than buying, and both avoid damaging a cold-water pump by asking it to do a job it was never designed for. For deciding what to buy for normal home cleaning, our <a href="/best-electric-pressure-washer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best electric pressure washer guide</a> focuses on cold-water machines that suit most driveways, decks and cars.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cold-water machine</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Hot-water machine</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water in the pump</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cold</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cold</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Where heat comes from</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Burner or element after the pump</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical output temperature</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Supply temperature</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Commonly about 180-200°F</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Best at</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dirt, mildew, general cleaning</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Grease, oil, heavy buildup</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical buyer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Homeowner</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Contractor or fleet</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="instead" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Should You Use Instead of Hot Water?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Detergent does most of what people want hot water to do. Heat helps cleaning because it softens grease and speeds up chemical reactions, but a cleaner formulated for the job does the chemistry directly. Apply it through the machine's onboard tank or the low-pressure soap nozzle, let it sit for the time on the label, and rinse with the pressure nozzle. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-use-detergent-in-a-pressure-washer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how to use detergent in a pressure washer</a> walks through the soap nozzle, dwell time and rinse order.
          </p>

          <p>
            Match the product to the mess. A degreaser made for concrete or engines will lift oil stains far better than hot water alone, and a house-wash product handles mildew and dirt on siding. Use cleaners labeled as safe for pressure washers, because some household products are too thick for the injector or contain ingredients that are hard on seals. If you were wondering about kitchen products, our piece on <a href="/can-you-use-dish-soap-in-a-pressure-washer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">using dish soap in a pressure washer</a> explains when it causes trouble.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dwell time is the part most people skip. A cleaner sprayed on and immediately blasted off has barely started working. Most need several minutes on the surface, kept wet and out of direct sun so they do not dry. On a hot day, work in smaller sections. That patience gets you most of the advantage that warm water would have given, with no risk to the pump.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a small greasy job, use hot water by hand rather than through the machine. A bucket of hot water with a suitable cleaner and a stiff brush will loosen a grill grate, a greasy step or a patch of oil-stained concrete, and the pressure washer can then rinse it cold. That split keeps heat where it helps, on the surface, and out of the pump where it does harm.
          </p>

          <p>
            Technique matters as much as chemistry. A rotating turbo nozzle or a surface cleaner on concrete, and moving closer with a narrower tip on stubborn spots, will often clear what you hoped heat would. Work from the bottom up when applying cleaner on vertical surfaces and top down when rinsing. If the job truly needs heat after all of that, it is a job for a rented or hired hot-water machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="already" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What If You Already Ran Hot Water Through It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Stop and switch to cold water. A short exposure, a few minutes of warm water, may cause no lasting damage, especially if the machine was spraying rather than idling in bypass. Run cool water through the pump for a minute or two with the trigger pulled to flush the heat out and let the seals recover. Do not keep using hot water to finish the job just because the machine still seems to work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then watch for the signs that something has been hurt. The most common are water dripping or spraying from the pump body or around the inlet, a pressure drop compared with before, pulsing that was not there before, or the thermal relief valve dribbling water during normal use. A sharp smell of hot plastic or visible distortion around fittings is a bad sign. Any of these after a hot-water session points to seals or valves that have been damaged.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some of that is repairable. On many machines, seal kits and unloader parts are available, and on triplex pumps a rebuild is routine. On a budget electric unit with a sealed axial pump, the realistic repair is often a complete pump assembly, and that can cost a large share of the machine's price. If the warranty is still active, read the exclusions before claiming, because damage from exceeding the inlet temperature is commonly excluded.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the machine is pulsing or surging after the incident, rule out ordinary supply problems first, because they are more likely and cheaper to fix. Our article on <a href="/why-is-my-pressure-washer-pulsing/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">why a pressure washer pulses</a> covers the checks in order, and our guide to a <a href="/pressure-washer-not-building-pressure/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">pressure washer not building pressure</a> handles weak output. If both come back clean, worn seals or valves from the heat are the likely cause.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Connecting to a utility sink's hot tap</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The water heater is usually set around 120°F, already above the common 104°F inlet limit of consumer machines. Running the hot side, or a hot-and-cold mix you judged by feel, sends water into the pump that is too hot for its seals. Use the cold supply and let detergent handle the grease instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming a gas machine is fine with hot water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bigger engine says nothing about the pump. Many gas pressure washers use axial pumps with the same kind of inlet limit as electric units. Only a pump specifically rated for warm supply, usually a quality triplex, can take it, and only up to the figure printed in its specifications.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the hose flush on a hot day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dark garden hose in full sun can hold water hot enough to sting, and the first minute of flow can exceed the pump's limit. Run the hose onto the grass until it comes out cool before connecting. It costs a few gallons and also clears grit before it reaches the inlet screen.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blasting cleaner off immediately</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                People reach for hot water because cold water with soap did not seem to work, when the real problem was zero dwell time. Most cleaners need several minutes wet on the surface to break down grease or mildew. Keep the area damp and shaded, then rinse with the pressure nozzle.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a hot-water unit for occasional jobs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hot-water pressure washers cost several times more, need fuel or a heavy electrical supply, and demand more maintenance. For a few greasy jobs a year, renting one for a day or hiring a contractor costs far less and leaves your cold-water machine doing the work it was designed for.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the maximum water temperature for an electric pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On many consumer electric pressure washers the stated maximum inlet temperature is about 104°F, or 40°C, and some list a little less. The exact figure is in the specifications section of your manual. If the manual gives no figure, assume cold water only, because a pump without a stated limit was designed around an ordinary garden hose supply.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does hot water clean better than cold water with a pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, heat helps break down grease and oil, which is why commercial hot-water machines exist. For dirt, mildew, algae and general grime, cold water with the right detergent and enough dwell time does the job well. The cleaning gain from hot water matters most on heavy oil and grease, not typical household cleaning.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will hot water void my pressure washer warranty?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can. Manufacturers list an inlet temperature limit because exceeding it damages seals and pump parts, and warranty terms commonly exclude damage caused by misuse or by operating outside the stated specifications. If you have exceeded the limit and the pump starts leaking, read the warranty exclusions before assuming a claim will be accepted.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use warm water in winter so the pump does not freeze?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Do not try to protect a pump by running warm water through it. Freezing happens while the machine sits wet in storage, not while it runs. The right fix is to drain the machine and draw pump antifreeze or a pump saver product through it after the last use, then store it somewhere that stays above freezing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do hot-water pressure washers heat the water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They take cold water into the pump like any other machine, then send the pressurized water through a heating coil before it reaches the gun. Commercial units usually fire a diesel or kerosene burner around the coil, while some smaller models use electric heating elements. Because the heat is added after the pump, the pump itself only ever sees cold water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a pressure washer pump overheat even with cold water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. When you release the trigger on a machine that keeps running, water circulates inside the pump and heats up quickly. That is why many manuals limit how long the machine can idle with the trigger released, and why many pumps have a thermal relief valve. Starting with hot supply water simply shortens that safe window.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a hot-water pressure washer worth it for a homeowner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rarely. They cost several times more than a good electric machine, often need a burner or a 240-volt circuit, and need more upkeep. If you face heavy grease regularly, such as engine equipment or an oil-stained shop floor, it can make sense. Otherwise rent one for the occasional greasy job and keep a cold-water machine for everything else.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

