        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A consumer pressure washer is a cold-water machine. Its manual will usually cap inlet water around 104°F, the water heater in your house runs hotter than that, and the pump's seals and valves depend on cool water to survive. Feeding it hot water is one of the quickest ways to turn a working machine into a leaking one, and it is commonly excluded from warranty cover.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you want the cleaning power that heat provides, get it another way. Use a cleaner made for the mess, give it time to work, scrub small greasy jobs by hand with hot water and rinse cold, and rent or hire a true hot-water machine for the rare job that needs one. Check your own manual for the exact limit, and when in doubt, keep the supply cold.
          </p>
        </div>

